# .trivyignore — suppressions for the GATING Trivy scans (CFG-WU-11).
#
# The CI Trivy steps run with --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --exit-code 1
# --ignore-unfixed=true --ignorefile .trivyignore, so a CRITICAL/HIGH finding
# that has a fix available and is NOT listed below FAILS the build (ci.yml job
# `trivy-gate`, mirrored in release.yml `trivy-gate-release`). A separate
# SARIF-upload step runs with --exit-code 0 so the GitHub Security tab still
# populates with the full finding set regardless of suppressions here.
#
# RULES FOR ADDING AN ENTRY (every suppression must be justified, dated, and
# time-boxed — an undated/forever suppression is a silent risk acceptance):
#
#   CVE-XXXX-NNNNN   # OWNER: <name/team> | JUSTIFICATION: <why not exploitable
#                    # / why accepted> | ADDED: YYYY-MM-DD | EXPIRES: YYYY-MM-DD
#                    # | TRACKING: <issue/POA&M id>
#
# An expired entry MUST be re-justified or removed; the expiry date is a
# forcing function for periodic review, not a permanent exemption. Trivy itself
# does not enforce EXPIRES — review it during the monthly vuln-triage cadence.
#
# Example (commented out — there are NO active suppressions today):
#
# CVE-2024-00000   # OWNER: platform-sec | JUSTIFICATION: not reachable — the
#                  # affected codepath (foo.Bar) is never invoked by either
#                  # shipped binary | ADDED: 2026-06-01 | EXPIRES: 2026-09-01
#                  # | TRACKING: POAM-123
#
# --- ACTIVE SUPPRESSIONS (none) ---
# Intentionally empty: a clean build must pass the gate with zero suppressions.
# A seeded HIGH/CRITICAL CVE (e.g. an intentionally vulnerable dependency) will
# therefore fail the trivy-gate job until it is fixed or justified above.
